// **The per-OS socket values, asserted on any host** (ADR-0205 §6).
//
// `modules/Socket`'s docs have named two platform differences since ADR-0158 and neither was
// implemented: BSD lays `sockaddr_in` out as `{ uint8_t sin_len; sa_family_t sin_family; }` where
// Linux has `{ uint16_t sin_family; }`, and `SOL_SOCKET` is `0xffff` on macOS against `1` on Linux.
// The comment even said what it would cost — "two lines, and wrong silently until then" — and macOS
// made the difference invisible for the module's whole life.
//
// **Why this program exists rather than a unit test.** Both routines take the operating system as a
// *parameter*, which is `modules/GL`'s convention (ADR-0184) and for its reason: a function that reads
// `os()` internally has exactly one executable path per machine, so the Linux branch would be code no
// test on this developer's machine could ever look at. Parameterised, every branch is reachable here.
//
// The size is *not* asserted, though the reason no per-OS struct is generated is that the two layouts
// are the same size: `size_of` does not accept a qualified type name. See the note beside the exit.
#import "Basic";
Sock :: #import "Socket";

main :: () {
  n := 0;

  // macOS: `sin_len` is the structure's size and `sin_family` is one byte after it.
  mac: Sock.Sockaddr_In;
  Sock.leading_bytes_for(*mac, Operating_System.MACOS);
  if mac.length == cast(u8, 16) && mac.family == cast(u8, 2) {
    n = n + 1;
  }

  // Linux: one little-endian `uint16_t sin_family`, so `AF_INET`'s low byte lands in the first byte
  // and the second is zero. The byte BSD uses for `sin_len` does not exist there.
  lin: Sock.Sockaddr_In;
  Sock.leading_bytes_for(*lin, Operating_System.LINUX);
  if lin.length == cast(u8, 2) && lin.family == cast(u8, 0) {
    n = n + 2;
  }

  // The two must actually differ, which is what catches the mistake a reader would really make:
  // editing one branch and leaving the other aliased to it.
  if mac.length != lin.length {
    n = n + 4;
  }

  // `SOL_SOCKET` differs, and both values are named rather than one being the host's and the other
  // unreachable text.
  if Sock.sol_socket_for(Operating_System.MACOS) == 65535 {
    n = n + 8;
  }
  if Sock.sol_socket_for(Operating_System.LINUX) == 1 {
    n = n + 16;
  }

  // **The host's own `SOL_SOCKET` is asserted in Rust, not here** — same reason as `137`, and found the
  // same way. `Sock.SOL_SOCKET` is `#run sol_socket_for(os())`, so it folds to `65535` on macOS and `1`
  // on Linux, and `os()` folds beside it; both land in this body's MIR and the snapshot is one file. See
  // `crates/jr-cli/tests/integration.rs` and ADR-0206 §8.

  // **The size assertion this program wanted is not writable.** `size_of(Sock.Sockaddr_In)` is
  // E0261, "`size_of` needs a type": a **qualified** name is accepted in a type *annotation*
  // (ADR-0179 §5) and not as an intrinsic's type argument. Found by writing this test, recorded in
  // ADR-0205 §6 as owed, and left out rather than worked around with an unqualified import — the
  // point of this file is the two branches above, and an import style chosen to dodge a compiler
  // gap would hide the gap.

  exit(n);
}
